1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,744
Your shop is busy,

2
00:00:01,744 --> 00:00:04,024
so you run two copies of the API.

3
00:00:04,024 --> 00:00:07,340
A customer adds an item to the cart on copy one.

4
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:09,759
Their next click lands on copy two.

5
00:00:10,309 --> 00:00:11,566
Where is their cart?

6
00:00:11,566 --> 00:00:13,327
If it lives inside copy one,

7
00:00:13,327 --> 00:00:15,277
copy two has never heard of it,

8
00:00:15,277 --> 00:00:17,541
and the customer sees an empty cart.

9
00:00:18,091 --> 00:00:20,312
Today you learn four ideas: state,

10
00:00:20,312 --> 00:00:22,533
which is what a program remembers.

11
00:00:22,533 --> 00:00:24,297
Stateful against stateless.

12
00:00:24,297 --> 00:00:25,342
Persistent data,

13
00:00:25,342 --> 00:00:26,975
which survives a restart.

14
00:00:26,975 --> 00:00:29,653
And why scaling out depends on all of it.

15
00:00:30,203 --> 00:00:33,595
You will watch a cart vanish, then fix it, with real commands.

16
00:00:35,645 --> 00:00:38,760
Go into the shop. Type: CD, cloud cart.

17
00:00:39,310 --> 00:00:42,360
Start it. Type: dot slash start dot S H.

18
00:00:42,910 --> 00:00:44,993
Add product one to the cart.

19
00:00:44,993 --> 00:00:45,811
Type: curl,

20
00:00:45,811 --> 00:00:46,331
dash s,

21
00:00:46,331 --> 00:00:47,447
dash capital X,

22
00:00:47,447 --> 00:00:47,819
post,

23
00:00:47,819 --> 00:00:48,711
the address,

24
00:00:48,711 --> 00:00:51,017
slash API slash cart slash one.

25
00:00:52,567 --> 00:00:54,464
The cart now holds one item.

26
00:00:54,464 --> 00:00:58,257
This API keeps the cart in a list inside its own memory.

27
00:00:58,257 --> 00:00:59,138
Read it back.

28
00:00:59,688 --> 00:01:03,250
Type: curl, dash s, the address, slash API slash cart.

29
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:05,187
There it is: item one,

30
00:01:05,187 --> 00:01:06,953
and the storage says memory.

31
00:01:06,953 --> 00:01:09,979
That memory belongs to this one running process.

32
00:01:09,979 --> 00:01:10,862
That is state.

33
00:01:11,412 --> 00:01:17,428
Now restart the server, as happens in every deploy. Type: dot slash start dot S H.

34
00:01:19,478 --> 00:01:22,315
And read the cart again. Same command as before.

35
00:01:23,865 --> 00:01:24,267
Empty.

36
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:25,405
The cart is gone.

37
00:01:25,405 --> 00:01:27,346
Memory dies with the process.

38
00:01:27,346 --> 00:01:31,630
Anything that must survive a restart cannot live only in memory.

39
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:36,795
Go into the shop. Type: CD, cloud cart.

40
00:01:37,345 --> 00:01:40,395
Start it. Type: dot slash start dot S H.

41
00:01:40,945 --> 00:01:47,239
Now run a second copy, on port five thousand and one. Type: dot slash second dot S H.

42
00:01:49,289 --> 00:01:50,067
Two servers,

43
00:01:50,067 --> 00:01:50,715
same code,

44
00:01:50,715 --> 00:01:51,947
same database file.

45
00:01:51,947 --> 00:01:55,255
A load balancer would spread customers across them.

46
00:01:55,255 --> 00:01:57,459
Add an item through the first one.

47
00:01:58,009 --> 00:02:03,940
Type: curl, dash s, dash capital X, post, the address, slash API slash cart slash one.

48
00:02:05,490 --> 00:02:05,815
Done,

49
00:02:05,815 --> 00:02:07,504
on instance five thousand.

50
00:02:07,504 --> 00:02:11,143
Now the customer's next click goes to the second server.

51
00:02:11,143 --> 00:02:14,002
Ask port five thousand and one for the cart.

52
00:02:14,552 --> 00:02:20,397
Type: curl, dash s, the address with port five thousand and one, slash API slash cart.

53
00:02:22,447 --> 00:02:22,824
Empty.

54
00:02:22,824 --> 00:02:24,833
The second server knows nothing.

55
00:02:24,833 --> 00:02:28,976
The customer lost their cart just by being sent to another server.

56
00:02:28,976 --> 00:02:32,303
A server that remembers things like this is stateful.

57
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:37,468
Go into the shop. Type: CD, cloud cart.

58
00:02:38,018 --> 00:02:40,747
The fix: keep state out of the server,

59
00:02:40,747 --> 00:02:42,543
and in a shared database.

60
00:02:42,543 --> 00:02:44,698
Start with storage set to D B.

61
00:02:44,698 --> 00:02:46,494
Type: storage equals D B,

62
00:02:46,494 --> 00:02:48,578
then dot slash start dot S H.

63
00:02:50,628 --> 00:02:56,942
Start the second copy the same way. Type: storage equals D B, dot slash second dot S H.

64
00:02:58,992 --> 00:03:01,697
Add item two through the first server.

65
00:03:01,697 --> 00:03:02,480
Type: curl,

66
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:02,978
dash s,

67
00:03:02,978 --> 00:03:04,045
dash capital X,

68
00:03:04,045 --> 00:03:04,401
post,

69
00:03:04,401 --> 00:03:05,255
the address,

70
00:03:05,255 --> 00:03:07,462
slash API slash cart slash two.

71
00:03:09,012 --> 00:03:11,977
Now ask the other server, port five thousand and one.

72
00:03:12,527 --> 00:03:18,372
Type: curl, dash s, the address with port five thousand and one, slash API slash cart.

73
00:03:20,422 --> 00:03:20,968
Item two,

74
00:03:20,968 --> 00:03:22,606
seen from the other server.

75
00:03:22,606 --> 00:03:24,791
Both servers read the same database,

76
00:03:24,791 --> 00:03:27,217
so it does not matter which one answers.

77
00:03:27,217 --> 00:03:29,766
Now the servers keep no cart of their own.

78
00:03:29,766 --> 00:03:30,918
They are stateless.

79
00:03:31,468 --> 00:03:37,954
One more test: restart everything. Type: storage equals D B, dot slash start dot S H.

80
00:03:40,004 --> 00:03:44,718
Read the cart. Type: curl, dash s, the address, slash API slash cart.

81
00:03:46,268 --> 00:03:47,026
Still there.

82
00:03:47,026 --> 00:03:48,921
The data survived the restart,

83
00:03:48,921 --> 00:03:51,131
because it lives in a file on disk.

84
00:03:51,131 --> 00:03:52,647
That is persistent data.

85
00:03:53,197 --> 00:03:57,080
See the file. Type: L S, dash L, shop dot D B.

86
00:03:57,630 --> 00:04:01,711
That one file is now the most precious thing on this machine.

87
00:04:01,711 --> 00:04:03,785
Persistent data needs a backup,

88
00:04:03,785 --> 00:04:06,997
and in real life a volume or a managed database.

89
00:04:06,997 --> 00:04:08,603
Servers can be replaced.

90
00:04:08,603 --> 00:04:09,406
Data cannot.

91
00:04:11,456 --> 00:04:13,141
Let us put the ideas together.

92
00:04:13,691 --> 00:04:19,025
State is what a program remembers between requests: a cart, a login, a counter.

93
00:04:19,575 --> 00:04:23,674
A stateful service keeps that state in its own memory or disk.

94
00:04:23,674 --> 00:04:24,533
It is simple,

95
00:04:24,533 --> 00:04:26,583
but you cannot just add copies,

96
00:04:26,583 --> 00:04:28,236
and a restart loses data.

97
00:04:28,786 --> 00:04:32,042
A stateless service keeps nothing between requests.

98
00:04:32,042 --> 00:04:34,086
Any copy can answer any request,

99
00:04:34,086 --> 00:04:35,490
so you can add copies,

100
00:04:35,490 --> 00:04:36,384
remove copies,

101
00:04:36,384 --> 00:04:37,470
and replace them,

102
00:04:37,470 --> 00:04:37,917
freely.

103
00:04:38,467 --> 00:04:40,348
The state does not disappear.

104
00:04:40,348 --> 00:04:43,073
It moves to a backing service: a database,

105
00:04:43,073 --> 00:04:43,592
a cache,

106
00:04:43,592 --> 00:04:44,760
or object storage.

107
00:04:45,310 --> 00:04:47,509
Persistent data survives restarts.

108
00:04:47,509 --> 00:04:50,356
Put it on a volume or in a managed database,

109
00:04:50,356 --> 00:04:51,326
and back it up.

110
00:04:51,876 --> 00:04:55,932
Tokens and settings work the same way: keep them outside the server,

111
00:04:55,932 --> 00:04:57,423
so any copy can use them.

112
00:04:57,973 --> 00:04:59,138
In real systems,

113
00:04:59,138 --> 00:05:00,230
docker volumes,

114
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:02,414
kubernetes persistent volumes,

115
00:05:02,414 --> 00:05:05,036
and cloud databases do exactly this.

116
00:05:05,036 --> 00:05:06,783
The idea stays the same.

117
00:05:08,833 --> 00:05:11,606
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

118
